{
  "summary": "Adversarial review of MM-03.3 pass 4 (C1-C9) in formal/Ern/MM03.lean, lens finders then refuting judges",
  "agentCount": 10,
  "logs": [],
  "result": [
    {
      "lens": "c1",
      "findings": [
        {
          "title": "The B8 clause of transfer now also covers dependents that move, which B8's text does not mention; the docstring cites only B8/C1 and nothing reports it",
          "severity": "minor",
          "location": "formal/Ern/MM03.lean, TransferPre (lines ~1145-1166), last conjunct `(AnchorMoves s src (s.effAnchor x) ∨ q = dst ∨ OwnedWithin s src q) → EffAnchorOK ...`; transfer_preserves_anchorOrder (~4880)",
          "cause": "design decision to report",
          "evidence": "B8 (task_mm03_p3.md) only says: \"`transfer` le préserve en rejetant un déplacement qui emmènerait une ancre dans un cadre plus jeune qu'un de ses dépendants utilisables\". It covers anchors that move. C1 says only \"`depend` et la clause B8 de `transfer` contrôlent l'ancre effective\". The step-1 change adds two more triggers, `q = dst ∨ OwnedWithin s src q`, for when the dependent moves. Pass-1 arbitrage 1 and A7 put the rejection of such returns at `exitFrame`. The commit message of eaf82ae does not mention the extension, and no p4 report exists yet. Lean check (scratchpad/rev8/c1/t1.lean, compiles): from `dependEff c10b 2 4`, every other conjunct of TransferPre holds, including the O4.6 `NoEscape` on movedDeps (anchor 4 has an origin), and `¬ AnchorMoves s 2 (s.effAnchor 4)` with `s.effAnchor 4 = 2`. So case 10 is rejected only by the `q = dst` branch. The same holds for n9d. In the pass-3 snapshot (p4/p3.lean:5852, 6012) both transfers were `ok` and the rejection came at the exit. This is not over-rejection. The clause is implied by `AnchorOrder (transferEff s src dst)` (checked in rev8/c1/t2.lean: `fun q _ hq x hx _ => h q hq x hx`), so it rejects only transfers whose `ok` result would break C1's AnchorOrder. Accepting them would contradict B8 ('transfer le préserve') and step 1 ('reachable_access_invariant_underDD conclut WF3 ∧ Inv3 complet'). MM-03.3.6 also forbids them: \"Une valeur dépendante ne peut être installée dans une destination dont le cadre survivrait à celui d'une ancre locale dont elle dépend\". Still, this is a reading beyond the literal B8, and it moves the point where cases 10 and n9d are rejected. The examples `ExitStep K0 c10c .reject` and `ExitStep K0 n9d .reject` now concern states that cannot be reached.",
          "suggested_fix": "In the p4 report ((c) decisions and (i)), say that the dependent-moves branch is required for transfer to preserve C1's AnchorOrder, and that it is MM-03.3.6 / O4.6 read on the effective anchor. Say that cases 10 and n9d are now rejected at `transfer` instead of `exitFrame`. Optionally cite MM-03.3.6 next to B8/C1 in the TransferPre docstring. No change to definitions or statements is needed."
        }
      ],
      "verdicts": [
        {
          "title": "The B8 clause of transfer now also covers dependents that move, which B8's text does not mention; the docstring cites only B8/C1 and nothing reports it",
          "verdict": "refuted",
          "severity": "minor",
          "reasoning": "The reviewer's facts are correct. The conclusion that this is an unreported interpretation does not hold, for four reasons.\n\n(1) The arbitrages require this behaviour, so the implementer chose no interpretation. B8 (task_mm03_p3.md) states the requirement as preservation: \"`transfer` le préserve\". C1 (task_mm03_p4.md) redefines AnchorOrder on the effective anchor, says \"Vaut pour toutes les ancres\", and says \"la clause B8 de `transfer` contrôlent l'ancre effective\". Step 1 requires `reachable_access_invariant_underDD` to conclude `WF3 ∧ Inv3` complet. The `ok` result of the case-10 transfer violates AnchorOrder (t1.lean, last example). That transfer therefore has to be rejected, and `transfer` is the only operation involved. The reviewer's t2 shows the clause is implied by `AnchorOrder (transferEff s src dst)`. So it adds nothing beyond what C1 and B8 already require.\n\n(2) The sources state the rule directly. MM-03.3.6: \"Une valeur dépendante ne peut être installée dans une destination dont le cadre survivrait à celui d'une ancre locale dont elle dépend\". MM-03.3.7: \"La dépendance d'un résultat créé depuis `arena_arg` porte sur l'ancre effective\". Pass 3 handled the dependent-moves direction through the O4.6 `NoEscape` on movedDeps. That worked because pass-3 AnchorOrder was literally `NoEscape` (p3.lean:1432). C1 widened AnchorOrder to effective anchors. Carrying the same direction over to the effective anchor follows the arbitrage; it is not a new reading.\n\n(3) Rejection at `transfer` contradicts nothing. Pass-1 arbitrage 1 and A7 state what `exitFrame` does. Neither says the transfer must be accepted. The expected verdicts (§11 case 10, \"Rejet\"; A-list \"sur un local de l'appelé via deux dérivations → reject\") give no step.\n\n(4) The change is documented. The TransferPre docstring states it: \"or if the dependent itself moves (the destination, or a place the value owns)\". `transfer_preserves_anchorOrder` already cites MM-03.3.6 next to B8 and C1. The cases section shows `TransferStep K0 2 0 ... .reject` for case 10 and n9d. The p4 report is a deliverable not yet written (formal/reports holds no MM03_v0.1_p4.md). \"Nothing reports it\" therefore describes unwritten work, not a defect in commits eaf82ae..HEAD. A commit message is not required to list decisions.\n\nThe kept `ExitStep K0 c10c .reject` and `ExitStep K0 n9d .reject` examples are true statements. They are kept under the step-5 instruction \"tous les cas des passes 1 à 3 rejoués\", so they are not a defect.\n\nThe reviewer concedes no change to any definition or statement is needed. The only remaining point is report content (case 10 and n9d moving from `reject` at `exitFrame` to `reject` at `transfer`). That belongs in the pending report, not in a finding against the module.",
          "evidence": "t1.lean compiles (`lake env lean` printed nothing). It shows: for `dependEff c10b 2 4`, every TransferPre conjunct except the B8 clause holds; `¬ AnchorMoves s 2 (s.effAnchor 4)`; `s.effAnchor 4 = 2`; `¬ AnchorOrder (transferEff s 2 0)`. So without the dependent-moves branch, `transfer_preserves_anchorOrder` (MM03.lean:4882), required by B8 and C1, would be false on a reachable state.\n\nThe first example of t2.lean compiles: the clause follows from `AnchorOrder` of the post-state. Its other two examples fail, but the finding does not rely on them.\n\nSources: B8, \"`transfer` le préserve\"; C1, \"Vaut pour toutes les ancres. `depend` et la clause B8 de `transfer` contrôlent l'ancre effective\"; step 1, \"conclut `WF3 ∧ Inv3` complet\"; MM-03.3.6, \"ne peut être installée dans une destination dont le cadre survivrait\".\n\nTransferPre docstring (MM03.lean, about lines 1145-1152): \"or if the dependent itself moves (the destination, or a place the value owns)\". The `transfer_preserves_anchorOrder` docstring cites \"B8, C1; MM-03.3 §3, O4; MM-03.3.6\".\n\n`ls formal/reports/` lists no MM03_v0.1_p4.md. The pass-3 report line 318 records case 10 as \"`reject` à `exitFrame`\"; that row is for the p4 report to update."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "lens": "c2",
      "findings": [
        {
          "title": "transfer_not_inv3_preserving was weakened to the pass-3 invariant even though the original statement can still be proved with the pass-4 Inv3",
          "severity": "major",
          "location": "formal/Ern/MM03.lean:6888-6895 (transfer_not_inv3_preserving, xferCex); commit f3ce37a",
          "cause": "formalization error",
          "evidence": "The step-2 diff changes `Inv3 xferCex ∧ TransferStep ... ∧ ¬ I3 (...)` to `(Inv3base xferCex ∧ AnchorOrder xferCex) ∧ ¬ NoDangling xferCex ∧ TransferStep ... ∧ ¬ I3 (...)`. The reason is that xferCex breaks NoDangling: its root 0 covers leaf 1, which belongs to the unusable part 1. Neither the commit message ('Inv3 gains NoDangling ... reachable_no_dangling') nor the docstring justifies dropping the stronger claim.\n\nThe stronger claim still holds. I checked this file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev8/c2/xfer_full.lean. It defines w := ⟨[1,0], 6, [⟨some 1,.frame 1,0⟩, ⟨some 0,.frame 5,0⟩, ⟨none,.place 1,0⟩, ⟨none,.frame 1,0⟩], fun _ => .uninit, [⟨.modify,none,[3],.frame 1,true⟩, ⟨.modify,none,[],.frame 1,false⟩], fun q => if q = 2 then [1] else [], ...⟩. Lean proves `theorem transfer_not_full_inv3_preserving : Inv3 w ∧ TransferStep K0 0 3 w (.ok (transferEff w 0 3)) ∧ ¬ I3 (transferEff w 0 3)`, and `#print axioms` gives [propext, Quot.sound].\n\nHow w works: place 0 has a parent link to the later index 1, which chain ignores, so places 0 and 1 are not leaves. The source footprint is therefore empty and NoDangling holds trivially. Place 2 is owned by the unusable part 1, so the transfer hands it to dst 3, and it becomes usable with the inactive anchor 1.\n\nThis file checks that w, like xferCex, breaks only the WF3 parts clause and satisfies every other clause, RootChain and RootHeld included: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev8/c2/wf_other.lean. It compiles with no output.\n\nSo the pass-3 statement 'transfer does not preserve Inv3 alone' is still provable for the pass-4 Inv3. The new theorem only shows it for Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder. This goes against the brief: task_mm03_p4.md says 'aucun énoncé affaibli, tout changement justifié'. It also breaks the pass-3 practice: MM03_v0.1_p3.md:424 says the witnesses that keep the text Inv3 'leur sens se renforce'. In pass 4, enter_not_inv3_preserving and exitFrame_no_dangling_needs_rootHeld kept the full Inv3, with unchanged statements.",
          "suggested_fix": "Put back the statement `Inv3 W ∧ TransferStep K0 0 3 W (.ok (transferEff W 0 3)) ∧ ¬ I3 (transferEff W 0 3)` with a witness that satisfies NoDangling, such as w above, together with its 'breaks only the parts clause' example. The xferCex fact (Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder ∧ ¬ NoDangling) can stay as an extra example if it is wanted."
        },
        {
          "title": "pinEff_preserves_noDangling has no docstring and no spec citation",
          "severity": "minor",
          "location": "formal/Ern/MM03.lean:5241-5244 (pinEff_preserves_noDangling)",
          "cause": "formalization error",
          "evidence": "The doc comment at lines 5234-5235 ('`write3`, `writeShared`, `pin` and `unpin` preserve `NoDangling` (C2) ...') belongs to `write3_preserves_noDangling` only, because a Lean `/-- -/` comment attaches to the next declaration. `theorem pinEff_preserves_noDangling` at line 5241 follows directly after the proof term of write3_preserves_noDangling, with no doc comment of its own. It is the C2 preservation result that reachable_access_invariant_underDD uses for pin and unpin (lines 5541-5547). AGENTS.md says: 'Every definition or theorem that encodes a spec rule cites the rule's location in its docstring.'",
          "suggested_fix": "Give pinEff_preserves_noDangling its own docstring that cites C2 and A8 (pin/unpin)."
        }
      ],
      "verdicts": [
        {
          "title": "transfer_not_inv3_preserving was weakened to the pass-3 invariant even though the original statement can still be proved with the pass-4 Inv3",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "severity": "major",
          "reasoning": "I checked each claim and all of them hold. Step 2 (f3ce37a) changed the conclusion from `Inv3 xferCex ∧ ...` to `(Inv3base xferCex ∧ AnchorOrder xferCex) ∧ ¬ NoDangling xferCex ∧ ...`. stmtdiff.txt lines 69-71 record this change. The new Inv3 is `Inv3base ∧ AnchorOrder ∧ NoDangling` (MM03.lean:1474). The new theorem therefore no longer shows that transfer fails to preserve the current Inv3 without WF3. It shows that only for the weaker conjunction.\n\nThe unchanged statement can still be proved with a different witness. I compiled the reviewer's w: it proves `Inv3 w ∧ TransferStep K0 0 3 w (.ok _) ∧ ¬ I3 (transferEff w 0 3)` with axioms [propext, Quot.sound]. Their wf_other.lean also compiles and shows that w breaks only the parts clause of WF3, as xferCex does.\n\nThe module has no other counterexample of this kind for transfer under the full Inv3. A grep for `¬ I3 (transferEff` finds only line 6894. transfer_preserves (line 2253) needs WF3.\n\nThe two sibling witnesses kept the full text `Inv3 s` in pass 4: enter_not_inv3_preserving (line 6494) and alloc_not_inv3_preserving (line 7298). The pass-3 convention (MM03_v0.1_p3.md (i)) is that these witnesses keep the text Inv3 and 'leur sens se renforce'.\n\nThe brief does not allow this change. task_mm03_p4.md 'Règles' says 'aucun énoncé affaibli, tout changement justifié ... contre-exemples conservés'. A1, still in force, says 'donner un contre-exemple pour toute préservation non démontrable'. The docstring explains why xferCex cannot satisfy NoDangling. It does not justify dropping the stronger statement when another witness satisfies it.\n\nThe change is not blocking. The docstring states the narrowing openly, so it is not silent, and no theorem holds because of a permissive definition. It is still an avoidable weakening of a statement and leaves A1 coverage missing, so major is the right severity.",
          "evidence": "stmtdiff.txt:70-71 OLD `Inv3 xferCex ∧ TransferStep ...` NEW `(Inv3base xferCex ∧ AnchorOrder xferCex) ∧ ¬ NoDangling xferCex ∧ ...`.\n\n`lake env lean rev8/c2/xfer_full.lean` prints \"'Ern.MM03.transfer_not_full_inv3_preserving' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]\" with exit code 0. `lake env lean rev8/c2/wf_other.lean` exits with code 0 and prints nothing.\n\nThe siblings keep the full invariant: MM03.lean:6494 `Inv3 s ∧ EnterStep ...` and MM03.lean:7298 `Inv3 s ∧ AllocStep ...`.\n\ntask_mm03_p4.md Règles: 'aucun énoncé affaibli, tout changement justifié ; ... contre-exemples conservés'. task_mm03_p2.md A1: 'donner un contre-exemple pour toute préservation non démontrable'."
        },
        {
          "title": "pinEff_preserves_noDangling has no docstring and no spec citation",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "severity": "minor",
          "reasoning": "The doc comment at MM03.lean:5234-5235 mentions pin and unpin, but it attaches only to the next declaration, write3_preserves_noDangling. `theorem pinEff_preserves_noDangling` at line 5241 has no doc comment of its own. I checked this in Lean with findDocString?: it returns none for pinEff_preserves_noDangling and a docstring for its neighbours. The theorem is the C2 preservation result for pin/unpin. Step 2 of the brief requires it ('préservation par toutes les opérations'), and reachable_access_invariant_underDD uses it at lines 5543 and 5547. AGENTS.md requires that every theorem encoding a spec rule cite the rule's location in its docstring. task_mm03_p4.md requires 'docstrings citant la clause ou l'arbitrage'. Minor: the omission is only in documentation.",
          "evidence": "Running a scratch file (rev8/c2judge/doc.lean) with `Lean.findDocString?` prints: 'Ern.MM03.pinEff_preserves_noDangling: false', 'Ern.MM03.write3_preserves_noDangling: true', 'Ern.MM03.transfer_preserves_noDangling: true'. MM03.lean:5236-5244: write3_preserves_noDangling's proof term is followed directly by `theorem pinEff_preserves_noDangling` with no `/-- -/` comment in between."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "lens": "c3c4c5c8",
      "findings": [
        {
          "title": "static_pinned_never_exits is vacuous on every reachable state: StaticPinnedBottom forces K.Pinned to be empty",
          "severity": "major",
          "location": "formal/Ern/MM03.lean:5350 (StaticPinnedBottom), 5368 (static_pinned_never_exits); root cause in S3.init (\"one frame, no place\") and enterEff (locals owned only by the new frame s.nF)",
          "cause": "design decision to report",
          "evidence": "StaticPinnedBottom k s := ∀ l, k.Pinned l → ∃ q, l ∈ footprint s.P q ∧ OwnedByFrame s.P (s.F.getLastD 0) q. In the model the base frame is always frame 0 of S3.init, and no place is ever owned by it. S3.init has P = []. enterEff gives each local the owner localOwner ls s.nF i, which is .frame s.nF (s.nF ≥ 1) or a place. allocP and transferEff only assign .place owners. exitEff never pops frame 0, since ExitPre requires F₀ ≠ []. I proved this in Lean, checked with `lake env lean`, axioms [propext, Quot.sound] only. File: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev8/lensC345/vacuous.lean. It proves `theorem staticPinnedBottom_forces_empty {k : K} {s : S3} (hr : Reachable k s) (hsp : StaticPinnedBottom k s) : ∀ l, ¬ k.Pinned l`, via `reach_base : Reachable k s → s.F.getLast? = some 0 ∧ 0 < s.nF ∧ ∀ pl ∈ s.P, pl.owner ≠ .frame 0`. So on reachable states the hypothesis holds only for an empty static pinning, and the conclusion `¬ k.Pinned l` then follows trivially. The theorem has content only on hand-built WF3 states that no kernel run reaches. Reading 'cadre de base' as the first entered frame does not help: it makes the theorem false, because that frame can exit. The case KD0 accepts `ExitStep KD0 (pinEff (pinEff c13a 0 true) 0 false) (.ok …)` with c13a.F = [1, 0]. The spec gives the initial frame its own locals (MM-03.3.1: \"Un cadre possède : … Un ensemble d'emplacements locaux\"), but the model's initial frame has none. The commit message and docstrings do not report this, and no p4 report exists yet.",
          "suggested_fix": "Do not change the theorem. Report in (n)2 that on reachable states StaticPinnedBottom is equivalent to K.Pinned = ∅ (citing the lemma above), and ask for an arbitrage. Options: let the base frame own places (locals of the initial frame), or restate the obligation against a frame that can own places and never exits."
        },
        {
          "title": "C8 is cited on the precondition EndUsePre, not on the effect endUseS/endUseEff that encodes it",
          "severity": "minor",
          "location": "formal/Ern/MM03.lean:3500-3515 (endUseS, endUseEff docstrings) vs 3563-3571 (EndUsePre docstring)",
          "cause": "formalization error",
          "evidence": "C8 (task_mm03_p4.md) is about the effect: \"`endUse p` laisse `p` lui-même utilisable, feuilles `Uninit` … Seuls les emplacements possédés par `p` deviennent logiquement morts.\" endUseS encodes it (I := … .uninit on footprint p; dead set only for places with heldIn s.P p pl.owner). Its docstring cites only A6: \"The value at `p` ends (A6) … the places owned by `p` or one of its parts are logically dead.\" endUseEff also cites only A6. The only C8 citation is in the EndUsePre docstring (\"C8: `p` itself stays usable, its leaves `Uninit`.\"), and nothing in EndUsePre's body encodes it. AGENTS.md: \"Every definition or theorem that encodes a spec rule cites the rule's location in its docstring.\" The behaviour itself is correct: I checked by `#eval` that `(endUseEff n6a 0).Usable 0 = true`, `(endUseEff n6a 0).Usable 2 = false`, and that endUse of storage 2 after unpin leaves 2 usable.",
          "suggested_fix": "Move the C8 sentence from the EndUsePre docstring to the endUseS (or endUseEff) docstring."
        }
      ],
      "verdicts": [
        {
          "title": "static_pinned_never_exits is vacuous on every reachable state: StaticPinnedBottom forces K.Pinned to be empty",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "severity": "major",
          "reasoning": "I re-ran the reviewer's proof and it holds. On every reachable state the base frame is 0, and frame 0 never owns a place. S3.init has P = []. enterEff gives the owner .frame s.nF with nF >= 1, or .place. allocEff/shapeOwner and transferEff only assign .place owners. ExitPre requires F0 != [], so frame 0 is never popped. As a result, StaticPinnedBottom k s with Reachable k s implies that k.Pinned is empty, and static_pinned_never_exits is trivially true on every kernel run. StaticPinnedBottom has no other user (grep: lines 5350 and 5368 only).\n\nThe theorem is not vacuous as stated. It quantifies over WF3 states, not over reachable ones, and its proof (unique ownership plus Nodup of F) has content on WF3 states where the base frame owns places. So the AGENTS blocking rule (\"hypotheses unsatisfiable\") does not apply literally. The definition is a faithful transcription of C5 (\"appartient a un emplacement possede, transitivement, par le cadre de base\"), and its docstring states the reading (\"the base frame, the last of the stack\").\n\nThe alternative reading does not help, as the reviewer said. With base = first entered frame, c13a (F = [1, 0], place 0 owned by frame 1, usable before the exit and not after) together with Pinned = {0} satisfies the hypotheses and falsifies the conclusion. I checked this in Lean.\n\nOne supporting claim is wrong. The initial frame having no locals is not a model deviation from MM-03.3.1. The pass-1 brief fixes it: task_mm03.md:20, \"depuis un etat initial (un cadre, aucun emplacement)\". The pass-1 report also states it: MM03_v0.1.md (c) 5, \"Le cadre initial n'en a aucun\". The spec only says each frame has \"un ensemble d'emplacements locaux\", which may be empty.\n\nThe real defect is therefore the combination of arbitrage C5 with the settled pass-1 initial state: the C5 obligation degenerates on every run. Cause: spec defect (brief), not a formalization error. No arbitrage settles this consequence. The step-3 commit message, the docstrings and any existing report do not mention it, and the p4 report that must list obligations in (n)2 does not exist yet. The code should stay unchanged; the point must be reported and arbitrated before milestone 1 closes. Major, not blocking, because the Lean statement follows C5 and is non-trivial on WF3.",
          "evidence": "Ran `lake env lean rev8/lensC345/vacuous.lean`: output \"'Ern.MM03.staticPinnedBottom_forces_empty' depends on axioms: [propext, Quot.sound]\", no errors. The file proves reach_base (s.F.getLast? = some 0, 0 < s.nF, every pl in s.P has pl.owner != .frame 0), then `Reachable k s -> StaticPinnedBottom k s -> forall l, not k.Pinned l`. Code: S3.init = <[0], 1, [], ...> (MM03.lean:267-268). enterP/localOwner use .frame s.nF (1204-1213). transferEff sets owner := .place dst (994). allocP = shapeP s.P (.place owner) ls (3928-3929). ExitPre requires F0 != [] (1290). My own check, file rev8/judge_c3c4c5c8/alt.lean, compiles with exit 0: c13a.F = [1,0]; c13a.Usable 0 = true and ({c13a with F := [0]}).Usable 0 = false; 0 is in footprint c13a.P 0; OwnedByFrame c13a.P 1 0; no place of c13a is owned by frame 0. Sources: C5 (task_mm03_p4.md:14) \"toute feuille couverte par K.Pinned appartient a un emplacement possede, transitivement, par le cadre de base\"; task_mm03.md:20 \"un cadre, aucun emplacement\"; MM03_v0.1.md:168 \"Le cadre initial n'en a aucun\". The step-3 commit 3834956 message only says \"StaticPinnedBottom and static_pinned_never_exits.\""
        },
        {
          "title": "C8 is cited on the precondition EndUsePre, not on the effect endUseS/endUseEff that encodes it",
          "verdict": "refuted",
